From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrew Druchenko <bananos@dev.co.ua>
Date: Sat, 28 Nov 2026 10:53:20 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] b43: N-PHY: TX A-MPDU on 802.11n cores

Take TX block ack sessions and send A-MPDUs on N-PHY cores with the
802.11n microcode. b43 builds them in its TX work from consecutive
frames of one station and TID inside the session's window, within the
station's length and density limits, and posts the members to the DMA
ring together, marked first, intermediate and last in their TX headers.
The microcode reports one TX status for an A-MPDU followed by the block
ack bitmap; the members not acknowledged go back to the head of the
queue a few times (at the fallback rate after two), then to mac80211,
which sends a BAR past them. One rate control sample per A-MPDU goes to
minstrel_ht. mac80211 of 4.19 has no TX queue scheduling, so b43 bounds
its own queues and stops mac80211's.

The 802.11n initvals leave the BE TX FIFO too small for an A-MPDU and
the PHY reports TX errors; size the FIFOs as brcmsmac does for core
rev 28.

A flow of TCP ACKs gains nothing from aggregation and the station's
reorder buffer holds them behind any hole, which costs an upload a
quarter of its rate; no TX session is taken for such a flow, and one
that turns short is ended.

ampdu_tx=0 turns TX A-MPDU off.

On an ASUS RT-N12 D1 (channel 11, HT20, ht=1): AP->client 12.0 Mbit/s
(10.9 with ampdu_tx=0), client->AP 21.9 (23.0).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
